        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 916,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Compares two arrays and says, with a single true or false, whether every element is equal to its partner to within a tolerance.\n\nTwo decimals that should be equal rarely are exactly: 0.1 + 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004, and a sensor at rest still jitters in its last digits. So == almost never matches decimals. np.allclose compares with a tolerance instead: two values count as equal if they differ by no more than the tolerance.\n\nUse it to ask whether a whole pose has returned to rest, whether a body has stopped moving (this frame against the last), whether a fit has converged, whether two matrices are the same.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.allclose <tolerance: float>\nDefault: tolerance 0.001.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe values to test (a list is turned into one). Every input is compared and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nWhat to compare them with, held: send it first. The same shape, or a single value to compare every element with.\n\ntolerance (float box, also an inlet):\nThe largest difference that still counts as equal.\n\nrelative (float box, also an inlet):\nAn extra allowance in proportion to the size of in 2: 0.01 allows a further 1% of it. 0 (the default) uses the tolerance alone. NumPy's own defaults are a relative 0.00001 and an absolute 0.00000001, too fine for most sensor data, so here the tolerance leads.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nall close:\nTrue if every pair is within the tolerance, otherwise false.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1.0 1.05 against 1 1. Within 0.001 the 1.05 is too far, so false; the second node allows 0.1, and then they all count as equal - true. A metro re-sends the values twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.isclose marks which elements are close, one by one. == compares exactly. np.all of a mask asks whether every element is true.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Compares two arrays and says, with a single true or false, whether every element is equal to its partner to within a tolerance.\n\nTwo decimals that should be equal rarely are exactly: 0.1 + 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004, and a sensor at rest still jitters in its last digits. So == almost never matches decimals. np.allclose compares with a tolerance instead: two values count as equal if they differ by no more than the tolerance.\n\nUse it to ask whether a whole pose has returned to rest, whether a body has stopped moving (this frame against the last), whether a fit has converged, whether two matrices are the same.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.allclose <tolerance: float>\nDefault: tolerance 0.001.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe values to test (a list is turned into one). Every input is compared and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nWhat to compare them with, held: send it first. The same shape, or a single value to compare every element with.\n\ntolerance (float box, also an inlet):\nThe largest difference that still counts as equal.\n\nrelative (float box, also an inlet):\nAn extra allowance in proportion to the size of in 2: 0.01 allows a further 1% of it. 0 (the default) uses the tolerance alone. NumPy's own defaults are a relative 0.00001 and an absolute 0.00000001, too fine for most sensor data, so here the tolerance leads.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nall close:\nTrue if every pair is within the tolerance, otherwise false.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1.0 1.05 against 1 1. Within 0.001 the 1.05 is too far, so false; the second node allows 0.1, and then they all count as equal - true. A metro re-sends the values twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.isclose marks which elements are close, one by one. == compares exactly. np.all of a mask asks whether every element is true."
        },
